#HELPS

0 SUMMARY~

MOVEMENT                            GROUP
north south east west up down       follow group gtell split
northeast ne northwest nw
southeast se southwest sw
run exits recall
sleep wake rest stand

OBJECTS                             INFORMATION
get put drop give sacrifice         help credits commands areas socials
wear wield hold                     report score time weather where who
recite quaff zap brandish           color description password title
lock unlock open close pick         gainlist teachlist
inventory equipment look compare
eat drink fill
list buy sell value
move climb flip push pull turn burn bomb feed play

COMMUNICATION
gossip leveling music q/a
shout quiet info yell
say tell gtell reply
emote pose note channels

COMBAT                              OTHER
kill flee kick rescue disarm        ! save quit
dirt-kick backstab cast wimpy       practice train
bash

For more help, type 'help <topic>' for any command, skill, or spell.
Type 'help KEYWORD' to see a list of all of the available topics.
Also help on: DAMAGE DYING EXPERIENCE NEWS STORY TICK WIZLIST GUILDS
If you are new here, we recommend that you read BEGINNERS.

The COMMANDS topic shows only the commands that your current trust level
allows you to use.  Mortals and heroes will see only mortal commands here.
Immortal-only commands are documented under WIZHELP and will not appear to
non-immortals.

See also: COMMANDS, WIZHELP
~

0 NE NORTHEAST NW NORTHWEST SE SOUTHEAST SW SOUTHWEST~
Syntax: <direction>

These are shorthand movement commands for the diagonal exits in a room.
Each direction functions the same way as typing the full diagonal, for
example "ne" moves northeast and "sw" moves southwest.  If the exit exists
and is open, you will step through it; otherwise you will be told the way
is closed or blocked.

See also: NORTH, EXITS, RUN
~

0 ENTER~
Syntax: enter

ENTER steps into a portal, vehicle, or structure that does not map to a
standard directional exit.  Use it for magic gates, wagons, boats, and any
special entry point described in the room.  Look for keywords such as
"portal", "gate", "boat", or "door" to identify what you can enter.

See also: RIDE, MOUNT, RECALL
~

0 RIDE MOUNT~
Syntax: ride <mount>
Syntax: mount <mount>

RIDE and MOUNT climb onto a mountable creature or vehicle.  Only mobs with
the MOUNTABLE flag can be ridden.  While mounted you move with your mount
and benefit from reduced movement costs.  Some areas restrict mounted
travel.  Use DISMOUNT to climb off.

See also: ENTER, DISMOUNT
~

0 CASTLE~
Syntax: castle
Syntax: castle <message>

Castle chat is the private channel for members of your castle.  Typing
castle with no arguments toggles whether you hear castle chat.  Supplying a
message sends it to every logged-in member of your castle.  Synonyms: CC and
"-".

See also: CHANNELS, CGOS, CC
~

0 IGNORE~
Syntax: ignore <name>
        ignore none
        ignore list

Adds or removes players from a personal ignore list.  Characters on your
ignore list cannot contact you via tells or channels.  The LIST option shows
who you are currently ignoring; NONE clears the entire list.

See also: TELL, REPLY, AFK
~

0 LISTEN~
Syntax: listen

LISTEN receives a brief ambient sound description of your current room:
wind through cracks, distant dripping water, faint movement in the
shadows, and other atmospheric details that add flavor to the environment.
The description varies by room type and current conditions.

LISTEN is a flavor command, not a tactical warning.  It does not reveal
hidden characters or notify you of nearby enemies.  Use SEARCH to
uncover hidden exits or traps, and SCAN to see what mobs are in
adjacent rooms.

See also: SEARCH, SCAN, LOOK
~

0 REPAIR~
Syntax: repair <object>

Requests repairs from an NPC repair vendor on worn equipment.  The shopkeeper
quotes and charges a gold cost based on the item's current condition and type
before restoring it to full durability.  If the item cannot be repaired you
will be told why.

Repair vendors are found in some guild halls, market districts, and weapon or
armor shops throughout the world.  Not all shopkeepers offer repair services;
look for vendors who mention repairs in their room description or greeting.

See also: VALUE, BUY, LIST
~

0 SECONDARY~
Syntax: secondary <weapon>

Equips the specified weapon in your off-hand if you have trained the
SECONDARY weapon skill.  This toggles dual wielding; to return to a single
weapon, wield your main-hand weapon normally.

Dual wielding with low skill imposes accuracy penalties to both weapons.
As your SECONDARY skill improves, the off-hand penalty diminishes.  Smaller
or lighter weapons are generally better choices for the off hand.

See also: WIELD, DISARM, DUAL WIELD
~

0 DUAL WIELD~
Dual wielding is the act of fighting with a weapon in each hand
simultaneously.  To dual wield you must:
  1. Have the SECONDARY skill (gain it from a trainer).
  2. WIELD your main weapon normally.
  3. Use SECONDARY <weapon> to equip the off-hand weapon.

Off-hand accuracy starts lower than the main hand and improves as your
SECONDARY skill increases.  Lighter weapons work better in the off hand;
very heavy or two-handed weapons cannot be used this way.

To return to single-weapon combat, WIELD your main weapon again -- this
replaces the off-hand weapon.  You can also use REMOVE on the off-hand
item, but WIELD is faster.

See also: SECONDARY, WIELD, DISARM
~

0 TELEKINESIS TK~
Syntax: telekinesis <target>
Syntax: tk <target>

TELEKINESIS (TK) attempts to move or lift a distant target using pure
mental force.  It can retrieve objects in adjacent rooms, push enemies
back, or interact with distant levers and switches.  Requires psionic
training and sufficient mana.  Difficulty scales with the weight and
distance of the target.

See also: CLAIRVOYANCE, PROJECT, PSIONICS
~

0 CLAIRVOYANCE~
Syntax: clairvoyance

CLAIRVOYANCE briefly lets you scry your immediate surroundings for
concealed exits or threats without moving.  You receive a momentary
mental impression of nearby rooms and any significant dangers within
them.  Range and clarity improve with your psionic skill level.

See also: TELEKINESIS, DANGER, PROJECT, PSIONICS
~

0 DANGER~
Syntax: danger

DANGER SENSE gives you a quick pulse of psionic awareness about nearby
threats.  It warns you if the current room or an adjacent area contains
a particularly perilous mob, active trap, or other significant hazard.
Useful for scouting before stepping into an unknown area.

See also: CLAIRVOYANCE, SEARCH, PSIONICS
~

0 PROJECT~
Syntax: project <direction>

PROJECT sends your spirit one room at a time in the chosen direction,
letting you scout ahead while your body remains in place.  You can look
around the projected location and step further.  Running out of mana or
losing concentration snaps you back to your body immediately.

See also: ASTRAL WALK, CLAIRVOYANCE, PSIONICS
~

0 SHIFT~
Syntax: shift <player>

SHIFT teleports you directly to another player's location if the
destination and your current room permit travel.  Many safe, private,
or magically warded rooms block shifting.  The target receives no
warning of your arrival.

See also: ASTRAL WALK, PROJECT, PSIONICS
~

0 CHANGES~
Syntax: changes

Displays a log of recent game updates, balance changes, new features, and
bug fixes added by the implementors.  Read this regularly to stay current
on what is new in the game.

See also: NEWS, MOTD
~

0 MOTD~
Syntax: motd

Reprints the message of the day that is shown each time you log in.
The MOTD is used to announce urgent news, maintenance warnings, or
temporary game changes that affect all players.  If you miss it on
login you can review it at any time with this command.

See also: CHANGES, NEWS, RULES
~

0 NEWS~
Syntax: news

Shows recent news posts from the game staff.  News covers ongoing events,
story updates, policy announcements, and other information that is relevant
to all players.  Older entries scroll off as new ones are added.

See also: CHANGES, MOTD
~

0 RULES~
Syntax: rules

Displays the official game rules and player conduct policies.  All players
are expected to read and follow these rules.  Ignorance of the rules is
not an accepted excuse for violations.  Contact an immortal if you have
questions about how a rule applies to a specific situation.

See also: WIZLIST, CREDITS
~

0 STORY~
Syntax: story

Recounts the background lore and history of the game world.  Reading the
story gives context for the setting, the major factions, and the events
that brought the world to its current state.  Useful for new players or
anyone who wants to understand the roleplaying background.

See also: CHANGES, NEWS
~

0 LORE~
Syntax: lore <object>

Analyzes an object to reveal its type, magical properties, level, and any
special effects.  Higher lore skill gives more detailed information and a
better chance to identify cursed or hidden attributes.

See also: COMPARE, IDENTIFY, INVENTORY
~

0 AFK~
Syntax: afk

Toggles an "away from keyboard" flag on your character so other players
know you are temporarily unavailable.  While AFK, auto-reply to tells is
suppressed and your name may display differently in WHO.  Type AFK again
to clear the flag when you return.  The flag does not persist across
logins.

See also: TELL, REPLY, WHO
~

0 DAMAGENUMBERS~
Syntax: damagenumbers

Toggles numerical combat feedback on or off.  When enabled, the exact
damage dealt or received is shown in brackets after each hit, for example:
  Your slash devastates the orc! [-47]
  The orc claws you! [-12]
Bystanders in the room always see only the descriptive message regardless
of their own setting.  The flag is saved across logins.

See also: SCORE, REPORT
~

0 CRAWL~
Syntax: crawl

Adopt a low crawling posture, allowing you to pass through narrow tunnels
or low passages that block upright movement.  While crawling, movement
costs extra points and your combat effectiveness is reduced.  Type STAND
or CRAWL again to return to a normal posture.

See also: JUMP, STAND, SNEAK
~

0 JUMP~
Syntax: jump

Leap over a pit, low barrier, or floor-based hazard in the current room
when the terrain supports it.  A successful jump avoids movement penalties
or traps triggered by walking.  Some rooms require JUMP to proceed at all.
Failing a jump in a dangerous room may result in a fall.

See also: CRAWL, CLIMB, LEVITATE
~

0 DELET DELETE~
Syntax: delet
Syntax: delete <password>

DELET is a safety guard for character deletion.  First type DELET alone to
arm the command, then confirm with DELETE <yourpassword>.  Both steps must be
done in sequence to complete the deletion.

Deletion is permanent.  The character's name becomes available for other
players and will not be restored by immortals.  All equipment and bank gold
are permanently lost.

See also: PASSWORD, QUIT, SAVE
~

0 PROMPT~
Syntax: prompt
Syntax: prompt <format string>
Syntax: prompt all

Customizes the prompt shown at the bottom of your screen.  PROMPT with no
arguments shows your current setting; PROMPT ALL sets a detailed default.
Placeholders such as %h for hit points, %m for mana, %v for movement, %g for
gold, and %r for room name may be used to build your own display.

See also: COLOR, COMPACT, BRIEF
~

0 QUESTION~
Syntax: question
Syntax: question <message>

Toggles or uses the Q/A channel.  With no message, the command toggles your
subscription to the Q/A channel.  With a message, it broadcasts your question
to everyone listening on that channel so other players can help.

See also: GOSSIP, TELL, CHANNELS
~

0 BANK~
All banking commands require you to be inside a bank room.  Available
operations: BALANCE, DEPOSIT, WITHDRAW, and CONVERT.

Notes:
  - The bank earns 1% interest per real day on your balance.  Interest is
    applied automatically while you are logged in (catching up for up to
    7 days of offline time).  Accounts below 1 platinum do not earn interest.
  - No fees are charged on deposits or withdrawals.
  - Pkiller characters cannot access banking services.
  - Your bank balance is retained across deaths and remorts.

See also: BALANCE, DEPOSIT, WITHDRAW, CONVERT, WORTH
~

0 BALANCE~
Syntax: balance

Shows your current bank account balance in all denominations: platinum,
gold, silver, and copper.  You must be inside a bank room.

See also: BANK, DEPOSIT, WITHDRAW, WORTH
~

0 DEPOSIT~
Syntax: deposit <amount> [platinum|gold|silver|copper]

Moves coins from your inventory into your bank account.  An optional coin
type may be specified; if omitted, gold is assumed.  You must be inside
a bank room.

Examples:
  deposit 100 gold
  deposit 500 platinum

See also: BANK, WITHDRAW, BALANCE, CONVERT
~

0 WITHDRAW~
Syntax: withdraw <amount> [platinum|gold|silver|copper]

Retrieves coins from your bank account into your inventory.  Same syntax
as DEPOSIT.  You must have room in your inventory to carry the coins and
must be inside a bank room.

See also: BANK, DEPOSIT, BALANCE
~

0 CONVERT~
Syntax: convert

Exchanges all your carried coins upward to the highest denomination
possible (copper -> silver -> gold -> platinum).  Handy before depositing
to keep your inventory tidy.  Must be inside a bank room.

See also: BANK, DEPOSIT, BALANCE
~

0 CASINO LUCKY DRAGON GAMBLING~
The Lucky Dragon Casino is located in Dresden's Market Plaza -- head south
from the plaza to find the gilded entrance doors.

Games available:
  SLOTS     -- Three-reel slot machine (cost: 1 gold per pull)
  BET       -- Hi/Lo dice game (1-500 gold per bet)
  ROULETTE  -- European single-zero roulette wheel (1-500 gold per bet)
  POKER     -- Video poker, dealt once, paid on hand rank (5-200 gold)

Type the game's name alone for full payout tables and instructions.
See also: SLOTS, BET, ROULETTE, POKER, BANK
~

0 SLOTS~
Syntax: slots

Play the three-reel slot machine in the casino.  Costs 1 gold per pull.

  Symbols: SEVEN, BAR, CHERRY, LEMON, ORANGE, GRAPE
  Payouts:
    Three SEVENS -- Jackpot!  Win 100 gold
    Three BARs   -- Win 50 gold
    Three of any -- Win 20 gold
    Two matching -- Win 3 gold
    Any CHERRY   -- Get your 1 gold back
    No match     -- Lose 1 gold

You must be inside the Lucky Dragon Casino to use this command.

See also: CASINO, BET, ROULETTE, POKER
~

0 BET~
Syntax: bet <amount> hi
Syntax: bet <amount> lo

Hi/Lo dice game at the casino.  Place a wager of 1 to 500 gold on the
outcome of two dice.

  HI: wins if the two-dice total is MORE than 7  (pays 1:1)
  LO: wins if the two-dice total is LESS than 7  (pays 1:1)
  Exactly 7: the house always wins.

Examples:
  bet 50 hi
  bet 200 lo

You must be inside the Lucky Dragon Casino to use this command.

See also: CASINO, SLOTS, ROULETTE, POKER
~

0 ROULETTE~
Syntax: roulette <amount> <bet>

European single-zero roulette at the casino.  Place a bet of 1 to 500 gold.

  EVEN-MONEY bets (1:1): red  black  even  odd  low(1-18)  high(19-36)
  DOZEN bets      (2:1): first(1-12)  second(13-24)  third(25-36)
  STRAIGHT-UP    (35:1): any single number from 0 to 36

  Zero (0) is green and loses all even-money and dozen bets.

Examples:
  roulette 100 red
  roulette 50 17
  roulette 200 first
  roulette 25 odd

You must be inside the Lucky Dragon Casino to use this command.

See also: CASINO, SLOTS, BET, POKER
~

0 POKER~
Syntax: poker <amount>

Video poker at the casino.  A hand of five cards is dealt from a standard
52-card deck.  The hand is evaluated immediately; there is no hold mechanic.
Minimum bet: 5 gold.  Maximum bet: 200 gold.

  Payout table (net multiple of wager):
    Royal Flush     (A-K-Q-J-10 same suit) -- 100x
    Straight Flush  (5 consecutive, flush)  --  20x
    Four of a Kind                          --  10x
    Full House      (trips + pair)          --   7x
    Flush           (5 same suit)           --   5x
    Straight        (5 consecutive)         --   3x
    Three of a Kind                         --   2x
    Two Pair                                --   1x
    Jacks or Better (pair J/Q/K/A)         --  push (bet returned)
    Anything else                           --  lose

Cards are shown as rank + suit letter, e.g. "Ac Kc Qc Jc 10c" = royal flush.
Suits: c=clubs  d=diamonds  h=hearts  s=spades

You must be inside the Lucky Dragon Casino to use this command.

See also: CASINO, SLOTS, BET, ROULETTE
~

0 BREW CONCOCT~
Syntax: brew <potion recipe>
Syntax: concoct <component list>

BREW creates a magical potion from a recipe if you have the required
components in your inventory.  CONCOCT is a related command that combines
a specific list of components into a potion using your alchemical knowledge
directly.  Both require alchemical skill and the right materials.

See also: SCRIBE, QUAFF
~

0 SCRIBE~
Syntax: scribe <scroll recipe>

SCRIBE copies a spell you know onto a blank scroll, creating a single-use
magical item.  You must have a blank scroll, a quill, and sufficient
knowledge of the target spell.  The spell is inscribed at your current
level of mastery.

See also: BREW, RECITE
~

0 SHOOT~
Syntax: shoot <target>

SHOOT fires a wielded bow at a visible target in an adjacent room.  You must
know the ARCHERY skill and cannot shoot while already fighting.  The current
bow system does not require or consume a separate ammunition object.

See also: ARCHERY, WIELD, SCAN
~

0 PICK~
Syntax: pick <direction>
Syntax: pick <container>

PICK attempts to open a lock on a door or container using lockpicks and
the PICK LOCK skill.  Success opens the lock without a key.  Difficulty
depends on the lock's strength and your skill level.  A failed attempt
may alert nearby mobs.

See also: OPEN, LOCK, UNLOCK, STEAL
~

0 JUNK~
Syntax: junk <object>

JUNK destroys an unwanted object from your inventory, permanently removing
it from the game.  Once junked, an item cannot be recovered.  Use JUNK to
clear inventory space without dropping items on the ground.

See also: SACRIFICE, DROP, DONATE
~

0 BACKSTAB BS~
Syntax: backstab <victim>
Syntax: bs <victim>

Strike an enemy from hiding, delivering a single devastating blow before
they can react.  The attack requires a piercing weapon and that you are
hidden when you initiate it.  Damage is multiplied based on your backstab
skill level; a higher skill means a deeper wound.  You are automatically
revealed after the strike whether it succeeds or fails.  You cannot
backstab a target you are already fighting.

See also: HIDE, SNEAK
~

0 BASH~
Syntax: bash <victim>

Hurl yourself into an enemy with full body force, knocking them to the
ground.  A successful bash interrupts your foe's next action and leaves
them vulnerable to follow-up attacks for a brief moment.  Failure means
you stumble and lose your own next action instead.  Two-handed and large
weapons give a better chance to knock the target down; smaller frames are
easier to topple than giants or quadrupeds.

See also: TRIP, KICK
~

0 BERSERK~
Syntax: berserk

Enter a battle frenzy that sharpens your instinct to kill at the cost of
your defenses.  While berserk, you deal more damage and gain offensive
bonuses, but your armor class worsens and you cannot use skills that require
calm or concentration.  The frenzy sustains itself while you are fighting
and fades shortly after combat ends.  Only warriors with sufficient martial
training can enter a true berserk.

See also: KICK, BASH
~

0 BLINDING~
Syntax: blinding <victim>

A precision strike or magical technique aimed directly at an enemy's eyes.
On a failed save the target is blinded, suffering significant penalties to
hit chance and losing the ability to see room descriptions or other
characters clearly.  The blindness lasts for a limited duration and can be
cured by spells or natural recovery.

See also: DIRT, CONFUSE, STUNNING
~

0 CONFUSE~
Syntax: confuse <victim>

A psionic or magical assault that muddles the target's thoughts, causing
them to act erratically.  A confused foe may attack random targets, forget
commands, or simply stand dazed for the duration.  Intelligence and mental
resistance affect whether the target can shake the effect.

See also: BLINDING, NIGHTMARE, TORMENT
~

0 CRANE~
Syntax: crane

Toggle the crane fighting stance.  CRANE adopts a balanced, evasive style
that improves your dodge and parry while sacrificing some raw damage output.
Suitable for characters who prefer to outlast their foes rather than
overwhelm them quickly.  Requires the crane skill.  Entering combat at full
speed or running out of movement breaks the stance.

See also: FISTS, IRON, ROLL, KICK
~

0 FISTS~
Syntax: fists

Toggle the iron-fist fighting stance.  FISTS adopts an aggressive,
forward-pressing style with emphasis on raw hand-to-hand damage.  You deal
more damage unarmed but take more hits in return.  Best used when you intend
to end the fight quickly.  Requires the fists skill.

See also: CRANE, BERSERK, KICK
~

0 DIRT DIRT-KICK~
Syntax: dirt <victim>

Scoop dirt, sand, or debris from the ground and fling it into an enemy's
eyes.  On a failed save the target is blinded temporarily, lowering their
to-hit chance significantly.  The technique costs no mana and relies on
your DIRT KICK skill; a higher skill means a better chance to land and a
longer duration.  Only works in outdoor or appropriate terrain rooms.

See also: BLINDING, BASH, TRIP
~

0 DISARM~
Syntax: disarm

Attempt to knock your opponent's weapon from their grip during combat.
A successful disarm sends the weapon flying to the floor, forcing the
enemy to fight barehanded until they pick it up or draw another.  The
chance to succeed is influenced by your disarm skill, relative weapon
sizes, and your opponent's dexterity.  You must be in combat and wielding
a weapon to attempt it.

See also: BASH, TRIP
~

0 DISMOUNT~
Syntax: dismount

Climb down from your current mount, returning to normal movement.  If
you are thrown from or killed while mounted, dismounting happens
automatically.  Some rooms or area transitions may also force a dismount
if mounted travel is not permitted there.

See also: MOUNT, RIDE
~

0 DOORBASH~
Syntax: doorbash <direction>

Charge a closed or locked door with brute force, attempting to break it
open without a key.  Success destroys the door; failure leaves you
winded for a short period.  The chance to succeed depends on your
strength, the BASH DOOR skill, and the door's construction.  Magically
sealed or wizard-locked exits cannot be doorbashed.

See also: BASH, PICK
~



0 HIT~
Syntax: hit <victim>

HIT is an alias for the KILL command and starts or continues combat
against the chosen target.  Use it when you prefer not to type "kill".
HIT does not carry the additional PK logging that MURDER does.

See also: KILL, MURDER, BACKSTAB
~

0 MURDER MURDE~
Syntax: murder <victim>
Syntax: murde <victim>

MURDER (and its abbreviation MURDE) explicitly flags your action as a
hostile assault against a player character.  Unlike HIT or KILL, MURDER
is always logged and counts as a pk-flagged attack.  Against mobs it
functions identically to KILL.  Use with care in populated areas.

See also: HIT, KILL, PKILL
~

0 KICK~
Syntax: kick <victim>

A quick boot to disrupt an enemy or deal extra damage during combat.
Kick costs no mana and triggers on its own timer, separate from your
main weapon attacks.  Damage scales with your KICK skill and your
strength.  The attack can occasionally knock a smaller or off-balance
opponent back a step, delaying their next action.

See also: TRIP, NERVE, BASH
~

0 MINDBLAST MIND BLAST~
Syntax: mindblast <victim>

Unleash a raw burst of psionic energy at a single target.  The blast
deals direct mental damage and, on a failed save, leaves the victim
disoriented: their attacks may miss wildly for a short time.  Mindblast
draws from your mana pool and scales with your psionic skill level.  It
can be used freely in or out of combat and does not require line-of-sight
to a room exit.

See also: CONFUSE, CLAIRVOYANCE, TELEKINESIS
~

0 NERVE~
Syntax: nerve <victim>

Strike a precise nerve cluster to temporarily cripple an opponent.  On a
successful hit the target suffers reduced movement or attack speed,
slowing their ability to act in combat.  The technique requires hand-to-
hand training or knowledge of anatomy; the higher your skill the more
effective and longer-lasting the effect.

See also: KICK, TRIP, BASH
~

0 NIGHTMARE~
Syntax: nightmare <victim>

Plant a waking nightmare in your target's mind, flooding their perception
with fear and confusion.  Victims who fail the mental save cower, flee,
or act irrationally for the duration.  The spell is psionic in nature and
is resisted by mental fortitude.  It is especially devastating against
foes with low willpower or those already suffering other mental effects.

See also: CONFUSE, TORMENT, ENERVATE
~

0 RESCUE~
Syntax: rescue <ally>

Leap into the fray to defend an ally under attack, drawing that enemy's
aggression onto yourself.  A successful rescue shifts the attacker's
focus from your ally to you, giving your companion a moment to recover or
flee.  The maneuver requires the RESCUE skill and works best when you
have higher combat stats than the enemy.  You cannot rescue someone if
you are already engaged with a different opponent.

See also: SHIELD BLOCK, BASH, FLEE
~

0 SMITE~
Syntax: smite <victim>

Channel divine or magical wrath through your weapon in a single brutal
strike.  The smite deals additional holy or elemental damage on top of
your normal weapon hit, and on a failed save the target is briefly
stunned or staggered.  Smiting consumes mana and requires that you are
wielding a weapon attuned to the ability.

See also: STUNNING, BASH, BLINDING
~

0 STUNNING~
Syntax: stunning <victim>

A focused blow or magical technique designed to leave a target senseless.
On a failed save the victim is stunned: they cannot act, lose their
dexterity bonus, and are easier to hit for the duration.  The chance to
land and the stun length both improve with your skill level and the
victim's current condition.

See also: BASH, SMITE, BLINDING
~

0 TORMENT~
Syntax: torment <victim>

Inflict sustained psychic or magical pain on a target, causing them to
writhe in agony.  Tormented victims suffer a damage-over-time effect
and penalties to concentration, making spellcasting or skill use risky.
The effect stacks poorly with other pain-inflicting debuffs.  Mental
and magical resistances reduce both the initial application chance and
the recurring damage.

See also: NIGHTMARE, ENERVATE, CONFUSE
~

0 TRIP~
Syntax: trip <victim>

Hook or sweep a foot to send your opponent stumbling to the ground.  A
tripped target falls prone, losing their next attack and suffering an
armor-class penalty until they stand.  Trip works best against bipedal
targets; quadrupeds and very large creatures are much harder to topple.
Requires the TRIP skill; failure makes you lose footing yourself briefly.

See also: BASH, KICK, NERVE
~

0 AQUEST~
Syntax: aquest

Shows the status of the automatic quest system.  If a quest is currently
active, AQUEST displays the objective, the target mob or object, the
reward, and the time remaining.  If no quest is running you will be told
when you are next eligible to receive one.

Quest objectives are assigned automatically by the quest system and vary
by character level.  Complete them for experience and quest points that
can be spent on special rewards.

See also: QUEST, TOPTEN
~

0 TOPTEN~
Syntax: topten

Displays the current leaderboard of notable characters ranked by pkill
score, quest points, or other tracked statistics depending on the current
season or game mode.  Use it to see how you compare with other players
and to identify top-ranked targets or allies.

See also: AQUEST, SCORE
~

0 SEARCH~
Syntax: search

Actively examines the current room for concealed doors, hidden exits, or
lurking traps.  A successful search reveals the object and describes it
briefly.  Your chance to find something depends on your intelligence,
the SEARCH skill level, and how well hidden the object is.  Some things
can only be discovered through careful searching, not normal movement.

See also: TRACK, HIDE, SNEAK
~

0 TRACK~
Syntax: track <target>

Attempt to follow the scent trail or footprints left by a character
across rooms.  If sign is found you will automatically move one step
in the direction the target went.  Repeat the command to keep following.
The skill degrades with distance, time, and weather; rain or magic can
erase a trail entirely.  NPCs with the HUNTER flag track back at you
automatically.

See also: SEARCH, HUNT
~

0 HIDE~
Syntax: hide

Attempt to conceal yourself in the shadows of the current room.  While
hidden you do not appear on the room's character list to non-sneaking
players and most mobs.  Moving, attacking, or casting a spell breaks
hiding immediately.  The chance to hide successfully depends on your
HIDE skill and the lighting level in the room.

See also: SNEAK, STEALTH, STEEL, BACKSTAB
~

0 SNEAK~
Syntax: sneak

Toggle silent movement.  While sneaking, your footsteps do not generate
entry or exit messages in adjacent rooms, making it harder for players
and mobs to notice you passing through.  Sneaking costs extra movement
per step and requires the SNEAK skill.  The toggle turns off if you run
out of movement or enter combat.

See also: HIDE, STEALTH, STEAL
~

0 STEALTH~
Syntax: stealth

Toggle the enhanced stealth mode.  STEALTH combines elements of hiding
and sneaking into a sustained stance that makes you harder to detect
overall, including by mobs that might see through ordinary hiding.
It requires a higher skill investment than SNEAK or HIDE alone and
drains movement steadily while active.

See also: HIDE, SNEAK, STEEL
~

0 STEEL~
Syntax: steel

Toggle the STEEL stealth stance.  An advanced self-concealment technique
that renders you nearly invisible to passive detection by other players
and mobs.  STEEL is distinct from HIDE in that it persists through slow
movement and requires significantly higher skill to maintain.  It cancels
if you attack, cast, or are struck.

See also: HIDE, SNEAK, STEALTH
~

0 LEVITATE~
Syntax: levitate

Toggle psionic levitation.  While levitating you float just above the
ground, allowing you to pass over certain terrain hazards such as lava
flows, shallow water crossings, and floor-based traps.  Levitation
requires psionic training and a small continuous mana drain.  It offers
no protection against falling damage from great heights.

See also: FLY, PSIONICS, TELEKINESIS
~

0 STEAL~
Syntax: steal <object> <victim>

Attempt to take an object from another character's inventory without
their knowledge.  Success depends on your STEAL skill, the victim's
dexterity and awareness, and whether you are hidden or sneaking when
you attempt it.  A failed steal is always noticed by the target and
nearby bystanders, flagging you as a criminal.  You cannot steal from
players in safe rooms or during active combat.

See also: SNEAK, HIDE, PICK
~

0 SHOVE~
Syntax: shove <victim>

Push a target out of the room in a random or specified direction.  A
successful shove can separate enemies from allies, break formations, or
force a foe into a dangerous adjacent room.  The target gets a saving
throw based on their size and strength versus yours.  Shoving another
player counts as a hostile action.

See also: BASH, TRIP, DRAG
~

0 ROLL~
Syntax: roll

Toggle the ROLL defensive stance.  While in this stance you improve
your chance to dodge incoming melee attacks by moving with the blows
rather than taking them head-on.  The stance lowers your own offensive
accuracy slightly and requires the ROLL skill.  It disengages if you
are stunned, knocked prone, or run out of movement.

See also: IRON, BERSERK, CRANE
~

0 IRON~
Syntax: iron

Toggle the IRON defensive stance.  Brace yourself to absorb incoming
hits rather than avoid them.  IRON increases effective armor and
reduces damage from each blow at the cost of slower attack speed.
Particularly useful when tanking multiple enemies or absorbing burst
damage.  Requires the IRON skill; cancels if you move or are knocked
down.

See also: ROLL, BASH, CRANE
~

0 MINDBAR~
Syntax: mindbar

Toggle the mental barrier that shields you from psionic and magical
intrusion.  While MINDBAR is active, targeted mental effects such as
CONFUSE, NIGHTMARE, and charm spells have a reduced chance to
penetrate.  The barrier does not protect against physical attacks or
area-effect spells.  Maintaining it costs a small mana drain per tick.

See also: PSIONIC, PSYCHIC, PSIONICS, TRANSFUSION
~

0 PSIONIC~
Syntax: psionic

Toggle the psionic awareness channel.  When active you receive passive
mental impressions from nearby sources of psionic activity, alerting
you to hidden psionicists, active mind-affecting spells, or psionic
creatures in adjacent rooms.  The sensitivity and range scale with your
psionic skill level.

See also: PSYCHIC, MINDBAR, PSIONICS
~

0 PSYCHIC~
Syntax: psychic

Toggle psychic resonance mode.  An advanced psionic state that enhances
all active mental abilities: MINDBAR becomes stronger, psionic attack
spells deal more damage, and your saves against mental effects improve.
PSYCHIC mode requires substantial psionic training and drains mana
steadily while active.  It cannot be combined with IRON or ROLL stances.

See also: PSIONIC, MINDBAR, PSIONICS
~

0 PSIONICS~
Psionics are mental disciplines available to characters who have remorted at
least twice (remort 2+).  Upon qualifying, you automatically receive 4
randomly chosen skills from each of 4 psionic disciplines (16 total possible
skills spread across the disciplines).  At remort 5 (final) all 16 psionic
skills are granted.

PSIONIC DISCIPLINES

  Telepathy:   clairvoyance, danger (sense), project (astral scout),
               astral walk, shift (teleport to player)

  Kinesis:     telekinesis (TK), levitate, mindblast, ego whip,
               mind leech

  Control:     confuse, nightmare, torment, enervate, transfusion

  Defense:     mindbar, psionic armor, psychic shield, psionic (awareness),
               psychic (resonance mode)

PASSIVE BENEFITS
  Remort 2+: access to psionic skills; immune to hunger and thirst.
  Remort 4+: recall always succeeds (no skill check).
  Remort 5:  all 16 psionic skills granted; unlimited carry capacity.

RESOURCE COST
  Most psionic skills draw from your mana pool.  Some toggles (MINDBAR,
  PSIONIC, PSYCHIC, STEALTH, etc.) maintain a small per-tick mana drain while
  active.

See also: REMORT, MINDBAR, PSIONIC, PSYCHIC, TELEKINESIS, CLAIRVOYANCE,
          MINDBLAST, CONFUSE, NIGHTMARE, TORMENT, ENERVATE, TRANSFUSION,
          ASTRAL WALK, EGO WHIP
~

0 QUIT QUI~
Syntax: quit
Syntax: qui

Save your character and cleanly disconnect from the game.  QUI is a
shortened alias for QUIT that requires you to type the extra letter to
prevent accidental disconnection.  Both commands behave identically.

You cannot quit while in active combat.  Quitting in a non-safe room
is allowed but your character will remain visible in the room briefly
before fully departing.  All inventory, equipment, and bank balance are
saved automatically when you quit.

See also: SAVE, RECALL, AFK
~

62 IMMORTAL~
This help topic covers miscellaneous immortal tools.  Type WIZHELP for a
complete listing of all immortal commands available to your trust level.
For staff communication see IMMTALK (:) and WIZINFO.  Immortal channels
and flags are logged; all actions with LOG_ALWAYS are permanently recorded.

See also: WIZHELP, PSTAT, WIZCHECK, FINGER
~

62 BEEP~
Syntax: beep <immortal>

BEEP sends a ping-style private message to the named immortal regardless of
whether they have the immortal channel muted.  Use it to get the attention
of an AFK immortal without broadcasting to the whole staff channel.

See also: IMMTALK, WIZINFO
~

62 TRANSFUSION~
Syntax: transfusion <target>

TRANSFUSION is a psionic skill that lets you sacrifice a portion of your
own hitpoints to restore a target's health.  The amount transferred
depends on your psionic skill level and constitution.  Cannot be used
if it would reduce you below a safe minimum HP threshold.

See also: PSIONICS, MINDBAR
~

0 ALIAS ALIASES~
Syntax:
alias <word> <command as a string>     (sets the alias)
alias delete <alias to delete>         (deletes an alias)
alias <word>                           (shows an alias)

e.g.: alias mm cast 'magic missile'
      alias delete mm

To see all the aliases you have, type alias and to see a specific alias
type: alias <alias name>.

There is a max of 20 aliases per character.

See also: COMMANDS, HELP
~

0 AREAS~
Syntax: areas

AREAS shows you a list of areas in the game.  Area names are shown with
recommended levels.  However, those recommendations are just a rough range
and it is a good idea to read road signs to determine if you are entering
somewhere that may be particularly dangerous.

See also: WHERE, RECALL
~

0 ARRIVE~
Syntax: arrive <string>

Sets the message that others see when you enter a room.  The message must
include $n (your name) and $T (the direction you arrived from).

  $T (arrive) = the north, the south, the east, the west, above, below

Example:  arrive $n strides in from $T.

See also: DEPART
~

0 DEPART~
Syntax: depart <string>

Sets the message that others see when you leave a room.  The message must
include $n (your name) and $t (the direction you are heading).

  $t (depart) = north, south, east, west, up, down

Example:  depart $n slips away to the $t.

See also: ARRIVE
~

0 AUTO AUTOLIST~
Syntax: auto
Syntax: autolist

AUTO (or AUTOLIST) displays the current status of all automatic feature
flags: AUTOLOOT, AUTOGOLD, AUTOSAC, AUTOEXIT, AUTOASSIST, AUTOSPLIT, as
well as PROMPT, COMBINE, NOFOLLOW, NOSUMMON, and NOLOOT.  When you create
a new character all automatic actions are on by default.

See also: AUTOLOOT, AUTOGOLD, AUTOSAC, AUTOEXIT, AUTOASSIST, AUTOSPLIT
~

0 AUTOLOOT~
Syntax: autoloot

Toggle automatic looting.  When on, you automatically pick up all equipment
from mobiles you kill.  When AUTOLOOT is on, AUTOSAC will only sacrifice
corpses that have already been fully looted.

See also: AUTO, AUTOGOLD, AUTOSAC
~

0 AUTOGOLD~
Syntax: autogold

Toggle automatic gold collection.  When on, all gold is automatically
taken from mobiles you kill.

See also: AUTO, AUTOLOOT, AUTOSPLIT
~

0 AUTOSAC~
Syntax: autosac

Toggle automatic corpse sacrifice.  When on, dead monster corpses are
automatically sacrificed after looting.  If AUTOLOOT is also on, only
empty corpses are sacrificed.

See also: AUTO, AUTOLOOT, SACRIFICE
~

0 AUTOEXIT~
Syntax: autoexit

Toggle automatic exit display.  When on, visible room exits are shown
every time you enter a room, alongside the room description.

See also: AUTO, EXITS, BRIEF
~

0 AUTOASSIST~
Syntax: autoassist

Toggle automatic group assist.  When on, you automatically join combat
if another member of your group is attacked.

See also: AUTO, GROUP, RESCUE
~

0 AUTOSPLIT~
Syntax: autosplit

Toggle automatic gold splitting.  When on, gold looted from kills is
automatically split among all group members in the same room.

See also: AUTO, SPLIT, AUTOGOLD
~

0 BRANDISH~
Syntax: brandish

BRANDISH activates a magical staff you are holding.  You must HOLD the
staff first.  Staves have multiple charges; each brandish uses one.  When
all charges are exhausted the staff is consumed.  May require the STAVES
skill.

See also: ZAP, HOLD, RECITE, QUAFF
~

0 QUAFF~
Syntax: quaff <potion>

QUAFF drinks a magical potion, triggering its effects immediately.  This
is distinct from DRINK, which consumes mundane beverages.  Potions have a
single charge and are consumed on use.  No skill is required to quaff.

See also: BRANDISH, RECITE, ZAP, DRINK
~

0 RECITE~
Syntax: recite <scroll> [target]

RECITE reads from a magical scroll, casting the inscribed spell.  The
target argument is optional and depends on the nature of the scroll.
Scrolls have a single charge and are consumed on use.  May require the
SCROLLS skill.

See also: BRANDISH, QUAFF, ZAP
~

0 ZAP~
Syntax: zap <target>
Syntax: zap

ZAP fires a magical wand at a target.  You must HOLD the wand first.  If
no target is given and you are in combat, the current opponent is targeted
automatically.  Wands have multiple charges; each zap uses one.  When all
charges are exhausted the wand is consumed.  May require the WANDS skill.

See also: BRANDISH, RECITE, HOLD
~

5 TEACHLIST~
Syntax: teachlist

TEACHLIST shows a list of guildmasters and what they can teach you for
skills and spells.  Each entry shows the trainer, their location, and the
skill or spell they offer.  Some skills require that you first gain the
group they belong to before you can practice them.

Use GAINLIST to see which skill groups are available for purchase, and
PRACTICE to improve skills once you have learned them.

See also: GAINLIST, GAIN, PRACTICE, TRAIN
~

5 GAINLIST~
Syntax: gainlist

GAINLIST shows a list of guildmasters and the skill groups or individual
skills they will allow you to GAIN.  Gaining a skill or group costs training
sessions (not practice sessions).  For a description of each group, consult
HELP GROUPS.  For a list of who teaches individual skills once you have
gained them, see TEACHLIST.

See also: TEACHLIST, GAIN, GROUPS, TRAIN
~

70 DNS~
Syntax: dns

This command will turn DNS lookups on and off.  Remember that with DNS lookups
turned off, sitebans on name hosts will be ignored.

See also: BAN, ALLOW BAN

~

70 GRANTPSI~
Syntax: grantpsi <player> [now] [skill1,skill2,...]

GRANTPSI is a level 70 tool to guarantee a player receives psionics. Without
"now" it flags the target for a 100%% success at the level-based psionic check
and optionally locks in a comma-separated list of psionic skills to award when
the check fires (default behavior rolls normally). Using "now" grants psionics
immediately, sends the unlock messaging, and applies the chosen skills right
away. Valid picks include: psionic armor, psychic shield, mindbar, torment,
ego whip, enervate, mindblast, clairvoyance, astral walk, shift, project,
telekinesis, transfusion, nightmare, mind leech, and confuse.

See also: PSIONICS, ADVANCE

~

67 FSAVE~
Syntax: fsave

This command will force all the players to save, but doesn't send them a message
when you do it.  This command is also invoked on reboots and shutdowns.

See also: SOCKETS, RESTORECHAR

~

0 BRIEF~
Syntax: brief

If brief is on, you will see only short descriptions of rooms when you
enter them and when you type LOOK.  Typing brief once, turns brief on; typing
it again, turns it off, allowing you to see full descriptions.

The default is for brief to be off.

See also: COMPACT, COMBINE, PROMPT
~

0 BUY~
Syntax: buy <object>
Syntax: buy <n>.<object>

BUY purchases an object from a shopkeeper.  When multiple items of the
same name are available, prepend the position index: 'buy 2.sword' buys
the second sword in the list.

See also: LIST, SELL, VALUE
~

0 LIST~
Syntax: list
Syntax: list <name>

LIST shows the items a shopkeeper will sell you.  With a name argument,
only items matching that name are displayed.

See also: BUY, SELL, VALUE
~

0 SELL~
Syntax: sell <object>

SELL offers an object to a shopkeeper in exchange for gold.  The price is
shown before the transaction completes.  The shopkeeper may refuse items
they do not buy; use VALUE to check first.

See also: BUY, VALUE, LIST
~

0 VALUE~
Syntax: value <object>

VALUE asks a shopkeeper how much they will pay for an item.  Use this
before SELL to confirm the item is wanted and check the offered price.

See also: SELL, BUY, LIST
~

0 CAST~
Syntax: cast <spell> <target>

Before you can cast a spell, you have to practice it.  The more you practice,
the higher chance you have of success when casting.  Casting spells costs mana.
The mana cost decreases as your level increases.

The <target> is optional.  Many spells which need targets will use an
appropriate default target, especially during combat.

If the spell name is more than one word, then you must quote the spell name.
Example: cast 'cure critic' frag.  Quoting is optional for single-word spells.
You can abbreviate the spell name.

When you cast an offensive spell, the victim usually gets a saving throw.
The effect of the spell is reduced or eliminated if the victim makes the
saving throw successfully.

See also: SPELLGROUP, SPELLS, GAIN
~

0 BOMB BURN FEED PLAY~
Syntax: bomb <target>
        burn <target>
        feed <guardian>
        play <instrument>

Some area puzzles react to a specific tool. BURN requires a lit candle and
can reveal passages hidden by vegetation. BOMB requires a carried bomb bag
and opens specially marked cracked surfaces or damages Dodongo. PLAY uses a Recorder, whistle,
or ocarina to trigger a nearby musical puzzle. FEED consumes suitable enemy
bait when a hungry guardian blocks the way.

These commands only affect objects designed for that interaction. Examine
the room and its objects for clues. Some Hyrule stones also require the Power
Bracelet before PUSH can move them.

See also: FLIP, MOVE, PULL, PUSH, TURN, LOOK
~

0 GAMBLE~
Syntax: gamble

GAMBLE plays the money-making game inside one of Hyrule's five gambling
caves. It costs 10 rupees to choose a concealed sign. The result may award 20
or 50 rupees, or demand another 20 or 40. The command only works in those
First Quest rooms.

See also: BET, SLOTS
~

0 CLIMB~
Syntax: climb <object>

In order to get between certain rooms, you must CLIMB a rope, ladder or
other object.  Read room descriptions carefully to find the object you need
to climb.

See also: JUMP, CRAWL, EXITS
~

0 OPEN CLOSE~
Syntax: open  <object|direction>
Syntax: close <object|direction>

OPEN opens a door or container.  CLOSE shuts it again.  Both commands
accept either a direction (to act on that exit's door) or an object name
(to open a container in the room or your inventory).

See also: LOCK, UNLOCK, PICK, DOORBASH
~

0 LOCK UNLOCK~
Syntax: lock   <object|direction>
Syntax: unlock <object|direction>

LOCK secures a closed door or container.  UNLOCK removes the lock.  Both
require the appropriate key in your inventory.  Without a key, characters
with the PICK LOCK skill can bypass the lock with the PICK command.

See also: OPEN, CLOSE, PICK, DOORBASH
~

0 COLOR~
Syntax:  color on/off/list/default
         color <color type> <color>

The COLOR command is used to toggle color on or off and to set colors for
the various channels.

Typing the word COLOR alone will toggle color on or off.

COLOR DEFAULT is used to load the default colors for the various
channels.  If you turn color on without having set defaults, you will not
see any colors as they will all be set to grey.

COLOR LIST shows a list of the available colors.

To set the color for an indivdual type of message, use
COLOR <TYPE OF MESSAGE> <# of color>
The color number can be obtained using COLOR LIST.
For example, to make all gossips appear in yellow, type:
COLOR gossips 7

See also: PROMPT, COMPACT, BRIEF
~

0 COMBINE~
Syntax: combine

If combine is on, you will see like items in your inventory combined on
a single line.  The number of items of that type you have will be shown in
parentheses in front of the item.

For example, if, with combine off, you see:
a big pot pie
a big pot pie
a big pot pie

with combine on you would see:
(3) a big pot pie

If combine is on, typing combine again turns it off, showing you the long
inventory again.  The default is for combine to be on.

See also: INVENTORY, COMPACT, BRIEF
~

0 COMMANDS~
Syntax: commands

COMMANDS shows you all the commands in the game.

See also: KEYWORDS, HELP, SOCIALS
~

0 COMPACT~
Syntax: compact

When COMPACT mode is on, your actions and the game responses are shown to
you without spaces between the lines.  Typing compact once turns compact
mode on; typing it again, removes compact mode, showing blank spaces between
lines again.

The default is for compact to be off.

See also: BRIEF, COLOR, COMBINE
~

0 COMPARE~
Syntax: compare <object> [object]
Syntax: compare <focus> <object> [object]
Syntax: compare profile

COMPARE evaluates two complete hypothetical equipment sets without changing
what you wear. With one object, it finds the item equipped in the same exact
slot. With two objects, both must be able to occupy a common slot.

The default OVERALL result adapts to your level, class, guild, learned spells,
weapon proficiency, and trained combat and defensive skills. It reports the
estimated edge in weapon damage, spellcasting readiness, survivability,
leveling efficiency, and utility. COMPARE PROFILE shows the priorities used.

Choose a focus to make that category the recommendation:
  DAMAGE   - weapon dice, accuracy, attacks, and relevant combat moves
  SPELLS   - mana, recovery, casting stats, and learning capacity
  DEFENSE  - hp, armor, saves, immunities, dodge, parry, and block
  LEVELING - combat pace, recovery, learning, travel, and experience bonuses
  UTILITY  - movement, recovery, useful affects, stats, and immunities

Results are estimates against an equal-level standard opponent over five
combat rounds. They include item affects and warn when an item cannot be used
yet, but unusual opponents and tactics can still change the best choice.

See also: LORE, IDENTIFY, INVENTORY, EQUIPMENT, SCORE
~

0 CONSIDER~
Syntax: consider <character>

CONSIDER tells you what your chances are of killing a character.
When you consider a character, you will see two lines of information.
The first line refers to the relative level of you and the character you
are considering; the second refers to the relative number of hit points
(based on max hp, not current hp).  Between these two you should have a
rough idea of whether or not it is worth attempting the fight.

See also: SCORE, WIMPY, FLEE
~

0 COUNT~
The count command displays the number of people (that you can see) logged
into the mud.  It also displays the highest number observed that day, if
it is higher.

See also: WHO, WHERE
~

0 CREDITS~
Syntax: credits

This command shows the list of the original Diku Mud implementors.

See also: DIKU, MERC, ROM
~

0 DELETE~
Syntax: delete <password>

Use the delete command to erase unwanted characters, so the name will be
available for use.  This command must be typed along with your password to
delete a character, and you cannot be forced to delete. Deletion is permanent.
You will not be restored for deleting yourself.

See also: QUIT, SAVE
~

0 DESCRIPTION DESC~
Syntax: description <string>
Syntax: description + <string>

Sets your long description to the given string.  If the description string
starts with a '+', the part after the '+' is appended to your current
description, so that you can make multi-line descriptions.

See also: TITLE, WHO, SCORE
~

0 DONATE~
Syntax: donate <object>

If you have items in your inventory that you no longer need, you may
wish to donate them for the use of other adventurers.  The donate command
allows you to do this even if you are far away from the Temple of Devota.
The donated item will be placed randomly in one of the donation pits -
either in the Temple of Devota or in one of the guild halls in Dresden.

See also: DROP, SACRIFICE, JUNK
~

0 DRINK~
Syntax: drink <object>

DRINK consumes a liquid from a drink container to quench your thirst.
Characters who become dehydrated suffer regeneration penalties.  Use
FILL to refill empty drink containers from a fountain or pool.

See also: EAT, FILL, QUAFF
~

0 EAT~
Syntax: eat <object>

EAT consumes a food item to satisfy your hunger.  Characters who become
hungry suffer regeneration penalties.  Some foods apply temporary magical
or status effects when eaten.

See also: DRINK, FILL
~

0 FILL~
Syntax: fill <object>

FILL refills a drink container with fresh water from a nearby fountain
or pool.  You must be in a room with an accessible water source.

See also: DRINK, EAT
~

0 DROP~
Syntax: drop <object>
Syntax: drop <amount> coins
Syntax: drop all
Syntax: drop all.<keyword>

DROP places an object or coins on the ground in the current room.  'drop
all' drops everything; 'drop all.sword' drops all items named "sword".

See also: GET, PUT, GIVE, DONATE
~

0 GET TAKE~
Syntax: get <object>
Syntax: get <object> <container>
Syntax: take <object>
Syntax: get all
Syntax: get all.<keyword>

GET (or TAKE) picks up an object from the ground, from a container, or
from a corpse.  'get all' picks up everything available; 'get all.sword'
takes all items named "sword".

See also: DROP, PUT, INVENTORY
~

0 GIVE~
Syntax: give <object> <character>
Syntax: give <amount> coins <character>

GIVE hands an object or a quantity of coins to another character in the
same room.  The recipient must be present and able to receive it.

See also: DROP, SPLIT, DONATE
~

0 PUT~
Syntax: put <object> <container>

PUT places an object into a container.  The container must be in your
inventory or the room.  The object must fit within the container's weight
and size limits.

See also: GET, DROP, INVENTORY
~

0 EMOTE ,~
Syntax: emote <action>
Syntax: , <action>

EMOTE broadcasts a custom action to everyone in your current room.  The
comma (,) is a shortcut for EMOTE.  Unlike fixed socials, EMOTE lets you
type any free-form action.

Example:  emote grins wickedly.    or    ,grins wickedly.

See also: POSE, SOCIAL, SAY
~

0 POSE~
Syntax: pose

POSE displays a random, class-appropriate emote selected automatically by
the game.  Each class has its own set of poses.  Use POSE for quick flavor
text without typing a full EMOTE message.

See also: EMOTE, SOCIAL
~

0 SOCIAL SOCIALS~
Syntax: social
Syntax: socials

SOCIAL (or SOCIALS) lists all built-in social commands available in the
game.  Each social is a named action you can direct at yourself, another
player, or no target (e.g. smile, wave, bow, laugh).  Type the social's
name alone or with a target name.

The following is a (not necessarily complete) list of available socials:
gack        kiss        eve         bounce      smile       dance
cackle      laugh       giggle      shake       puke        growl
scream      comfort     sigh        sulk        hug         snuggle
cuddle      nuzzle      cry         poke        accuse      grin
bow         applaud     blush       burp        chuckle     clap
cough       curtsey     fart        flip        fondle      frown
gasp        glare       groan       grope       hiccup      lick
love        moan        nibble      pout        ruffle      shiver
shrug       sing        slap        smirk       snap        sneeze
snicker     sniff       snore       squeeze     stare       strut
thank       twiddle     wave        whistle     wiggle      wink
yawn        snowball    french      comb        massage     tickle
pat         curse       pray        beg         cringe      daydream
fume        grovel      hop         nudge       ponder      punch
snarl       spank       hand        yodel       faint       pinch
stroke      apologize   caress      stagger     snort       slobber
blink       tease       knee        flirt       tip         lust
flutter     bark        howl        babble      ramble      hush
threaten    roll        swoon       bird        eyebrow     serenade
grimace     boggle      beckon      wonder      worry       drool
nod         purr        point       rub         bleed       highfive
propose     peer        worship     bearhug     innocent    collapse
stretch     spam        boast       squirm      moo         moon
goose       wince       type        brb         mutter      rofl
sob         pant        whine       flex        embrace     duck
bonk        squeal      tackle      spit        life        mosh
flinch      air         tweak       peck        explode     raspberry
flash       strip       undress     tongue      view        grumble
cheer       plead       charge      criticize   run         judge
insane      cover       flare       head        pie         cower
noogie      yeehaw      pissed      passout     adjust      scratch
meditate    bkiss       beer        bcatch      claw        rose
laces       tag         tank        starve      aargh       homework
puff        differ      yae         lightbulb   voodoo      awkward
dab         derp        facepalm    micdrop     panic       slowclap

See also: EMOTE, POSE
~

0 EQUIPMENT~
Syntax: equipment

Lists all items you currently have equipped: worn armor, wielded weapons,
held items, and any light source.  Each item is shown at its wear location.
Use INVENTORY to see items you are carrying but not yet equipped.

See also: INVENTORY, WEAR, WIELD, HOLD, REMOVE
~

0 INVENTORY~
Syntax: inventory

Lists all items you are carrying in your pack but not currently equipped.
Use COMBINE to view stacked identical items as a single line.  Use
EQUIPMENT to see what you have worn or wielded.

See also: EQUIPMENT, COMBINE, COMPARE, LORE
~

0 LOOK READ~
Syntax: look
Syntax: look room
Syntax: look <object>
Syntax: look <character>
Syntax: look <direction>
Syntax: look <keyword>
Syntax: look in <container>
Syntax: look in <corpse>
Syntax: read <map or compass>

LOOK examines whatever you specify.  Looking at a character shows their
description and equipment.  LOOK alone describes the room you are in;
with BRIEF on, only the short description is shown.  Use 'look room' to
see the full, unbriefed room description.

READ normally behaves like LOOK. Some magical maps have a readable route
chart. A dungeon compass can be read while inside its matching labyrinth to
show the first general direction toward the boss and whether the chamber feels
close or far away. If you carry several, include the dungeon name, such as
'read eagle compass'.

See also: EXAMINE, SEARCH, INVENTORY
~

0 EXAMINE~
Syntax: examine <container>
Syntax: examine <corpse>

EXAMINE is shorthand for 'LOOK <container>' followed by 'LOOK IN
<container>'.  It shows the container itself and then lists all of its
contents in one step.  Particularly useful for inspecting corpses and packs.

See also: LOOK, INVENTORY, GET
~

51 EXCHANGE~
Syntax: exchange

If you are a hero, you may exchange experience for more practices.  The cost
is 5000 experience points.  This means that if you would rather get more
practices rather than another level, exchange the experience before leveling.
Otherwise you'll have to get 5000 more experience up and above your new level.
Exchanging experience for practices gives you 4-6 more; the exact number of
practices you get is random.  You may exchange only at the guru in Hero Hall.

See also: PRACTICE, GAIN, TRAIN, WORTH
~

0 EXITS~
Syntax: exits

Tells you the visible exits of the room you are in.  Closed doors are shown
in parentheses.  Not all exits are visible;  room descriptions will generally
provide some clues as to the existence of hidden exits and their names.

See also: AUTOEXIT, SEARCH, LOOK
~

0 FLEE~
Syntax: flee

Once you start a fight, you can't just walk away from it.  If the fight
is not going well, you can attempt to FLEE, or another character can
RESCUE you.  (You can also RECALL, but this is less likely to work,
and costs more experience points, then fleeing).

If you lose your link during a fight, then your character will keep
fighting, and will attempt to RECALL from time to time.  Your chances
of making the recall are reduced, and you will lose much more experience.

WIMPY will cause you to flee automatically if your hit points drop too low.

See also: RESCUE, RECALL, WIMPY
~

0 FLIP MOVE PULL PUSH TURN~
Syntax: flip <object>
        move <object>
        pull <object>
        push <object>
        turn <object>

Some rooms contain objects which need to be manipulated by flipping, moving,
pushing, pulling or turning them.  Manipulating these objects can have
various effects.  For example, one might push a lever or flip a switch to
open a secret door.  Read room descriptions carefully in order to find
objects that you can manipulate.

See also: LOOK, SEARCH, OPEN, CLIMB
~

0 FOLLOW GROUP ~
Syntax: follow <character>
Syntax: group
Syntax: group <character>

FOLLOW starts you following another character.  To stop following anyone else,
just FOLLOW <yourself>.  To keep yourself from being followed, use the
NOFOLLOW command.

GROUP <character> makes someone who is following you a member of your group.
Group members share experience points from kills and may use the GTELL and
SPLIT commands.  If anyone in your group is attacked, you will automatically
join the fight.

If the <character> is already a member of your group, then GROUP will
kick the <character> out of your group.  Characters can also leave your
group by using a FOLLOW command to stop following you.

GROUP with no argument shows statistics for each character in your group.

You may FOLLOW anyone but you may only GROUP with characters within 8 levels
of yourself.

See also: GTELL, SPLIT, NOFOLLOW, AUTOASSIST
~

0 GAIN~
Syntax: gain list
        gain <skill or group>
        gain convert

The gain command is used to learn new skills, once the proper trainer has
been found.  The following options can be used with gain:

gain list:    list all groups and skills that can be learned
gain <name>:  add a skill or skill group, at the listed cost
gain convert: turns 10 practices into one training session

Gain uses training sessions, not practices. (see 'help train').

Read the helps on the various spellgroups and skills to determine what
you want to gain.  The helps will show you the minimum level you need to be
to use a skill or the lowest level spell in a spellgroup.

See also: TRAIN, PRACTICE, GROUPS, TEACHLIST, GAINLIST
~

0 GROUPS~
Syntax: groups

The GROUPS command shows which spell groups you have (both default and
groups that you have gained).

See also: GAIN, SKILLS, SPELLS
~

0 HEAL~
Syntax: heal
        heal <spell>

The Temple of Devota and some other locations provide healers for the
assistance of adventurers.  The healer's services are generally free to
players of level 10 or below.  Higher level adventurers may be healed of
their wounds and ills on a fee for service basis.  The HEAL command without
an argument shows a list of the healer's services.  To receive healing, be
sure you have sufficient money and type heal <name of spell>.

For information on the heal spell used by those of the cleric class (and
members of the cleric's guild), read help for HEALSPELL.

See also: SCORE, AFFECT, RESTORE
~

0 HELP~
Syntax: help
Syntax: help <keyword>

HELP without any arguments shows a one-page command summary.

HELP <keyword> shows a page of help on that keyword.  The keywords include
all the commands, spells, and skills listed in the game.  Type HELP INDEX
for an alphabetical listing of all keywords.

See also: KEYWORDS, COMMANDS, SOCIALS
~

0 WEAR~
Syntax: wear <object>
Syntax: wear all

WEAR equips a piece of armor or clothing at its appropriate body slot.
You may not be able to wear an item if its alignment does not match yours,
it is too heavy for you, or you are not experienced enough.  WEAR ALL
attempts to equip every suitable item in your inventory at once.

See also: WIELD, HOLD, REMOVE, EQUIPMENT
~

0 WIELD~
Syntax: wield <object>

WIELD equips a weapon in your main hand.  Characters with the SECONDARY
skill can equip a second weapon in the off hand using the SECONDARY
command.

See also: WEAR, HOLD, REMOVE, SECONDARY, DUAL WIELD
~

0 HOLD~
Syntax: hold <object>

HOLD equips a light source, wand, or staff in the held-item slot.  Only
items designated for holding can be used this way.

See also: WEAR, WIELD, ZAP, BRANDISH, REMOVE
~

0 REMOVE~
Syntax: remove <object>
Syntax: remove all

REMOVE takes an equipped item and puts it back into your inventory.
REMOVE ALL strips all your gear except for cursed items, which cannot
be removed until the curse is lifted.

See also: WEAR, WIELD, HOLD, EQUIPMENT
~

0 JOIN~
Syntax: join <guildname>

The JOIN command is used to join a guild.  It works only when you are in
Melancholy's treehouse.  The possible guildnames are cleric, mage, thief,
and warrior.  Joining a guild costs 500 coins, which Melancholy will
take automatically when you use the join command.

See also: GUILDS, GAIN, GROUPS
~

0 KILL~
Syntax: kill <name of mob>

The KILL command starts a fight with a mobile (non-player character).  You
need to type kill only once per fight.  Attacking player characters
requires MURDER instead.

See also: MURDER, HIT, FLEE, WIMPY
~

0 PKILL~
Syntax: pkill <password>

PKILL is a permanent, irreversible setting.  Between level 15 and 30, you
may choose to participate in player killing by typing PKILL followed by
your password.  After level 30 the option is no longer available.  Once
set, pkill status cannot be revoked under any circumstances.

You can identify other pkillers only by walking up to them and using
CONSIDER.  Non-pkillers may only engage in player combat inside the arena.

See also: MURDER, ARENA, KILL
~

0 NOFOLLOW  NOFOL~

Syntax: nofollow
        nofol

NOFOLLOW toggles the follow flag, either on or off.

If you have pets you need to get rid of, or people you don't want following
you (i.e. members of a castle with whom you are at war), you can use this
command to prevent them from following you.

In order to be grouped, you must be able to accept followers.

The default for NOFOL is OFF (meaning you can accept followers), and you can
check the status of the NOFOL flag with the AUTO command.

See also: GROUP, FOLLOW, AUTO
~

0 NOLOOT~

Syntax: noloot

NOLOOT toggles whether or not anyone can mess with your corpse.
There are valid reasons why you might want someone to mess with your corpse:
to retrieve it or to get certain items from it.  If your corpse is set NOLOOT
then it cannot be picked up by, nor can objects be removed by, anyone else.

The default for NOLOOT is on (i.e. your corpse cannot be looted).  You can
check the status of this flag with the AUTO command.

See also: AUTO, NOSUMMON, NOFOLLOW
~

0 NOSUMMON~

NOSUMMON toggles whether or not you can be summoned, gated to, or cancelled
by other players.  Read help on SUMMON, GATE and CANCEL for more information
on these spells.  In general, you will want to turn NOSUMMON off only to
permit trusted wizard friends to assist you in transportation and in removing
harmful spells.

The default for NOSUMMON is on (you cannot be summoned, gated to or cancelled).
The status of this flag can be checked with the AUTO command.

See also: AUTO, NOLOOT, NOFOLLOW
~

0 NOTE NOTES~
The following options are available:

reading notes:
note list       : show all notes (unread notes are marked with an N)
note read       : either by number, or read next to read unread notes
note remove     : removes a note that you wrote or are the target of

writing new notes:
note to         : sets the to line for a new note (name(s), immortal, all,
		  or the castle(s) name)
note subject    : sets the subject for a new note
note +          : adds a line to a new note (i.e. note + hi guys!)
note -          : deletes the last line you typed (no argument required)
note clear      : erase a note in progress
note show       : shows the note you are working on
note post       : posts a new note. This MUST be done to post a note.

When you log in, you will be informed if there are unread notes waiting. Type
note read (or just note) to scroll through them one at a time.

You must be at least level 2 to use the note system.

See also: BUG, IDEA, TYPO, CHANNELS
~

0 ORDER~
Syntax: order <character> <command>
Syntax: order all <command>

ORDER orders one or all of your charmed followers (including pets) to
perform any command.  The command may have arguments.  You are responsible
for the actions of your followers, and others who attack your followers
will incur the same penalty as if they attacked you directly.

Most charmed creatures lose their aggressive nature (while charmed).  However,
they will become aggressive again when the charm wears off.  For that reason,
charmed aggressive mobs should not be brought outside the areas they are
normally found in.

See also: GROUP, FOLLOW, CHARM PERSON
~

0 OUTFIT~
Syntax: outfit

The outfit command, usable by levels 5 and below, equips your character with
a new set of sub issue gear (banner, weapon, helmet, shield, and vest),
courtesy of the Mayor's warehouses.  Only empty equipment slots are affected.

See also: EQUIPMENT, WEAR, SUBISSUE
~

0 PASSWORD~
Syntax: password <old-password> <new-password>

PASSWORD changes your character's password.  The first argument must be
your old password.  The second argument is your new password.

The PASSWORD command is protected against being snooped or logged.

See also: DELETE, QUIT, SAVE
~

0 PRACTICE~
Syntax: PRACTICE
Syntax: PRACTICE <skill|spell>

PRACTICE without an argument tells you your current ability level in all
the skills and spells available to you.  You can check this anywhere.

PRACTICE with an argument practice that skill or spell.  Your learning
percentage varies from 1% (unlearned) to a maximum of 75%.  You will continue
to improve in a skill or spell by using it as long as you are not at 1%.
You must be at a guild master to practice.

The higher your intelligence, the more you will learn at each practice
session.  The higher your wisdom, the more practice sessions you will gain
each time you gain a level.  Unused sessions are saved until you do use them.

See also: TRAIN, GAIN, SKILLS, SPELLS
~

0 SAVE~
Syntax: save

Immediately saves your character and carried inventory to disk.  The game
also auto-saves every 15 minutes, so manual saves are rarely critical.
Typing save can cause a brief command delay, so avoid spamming it.
You must be at least level 3 to save.

Note: some objects (keys, items more than two levels above yours) are not
saved and will vanish on logout.  QUIT performs an automatic save, so
you do not need to save before quitting.

See also: QUIT, PASSWORD
~

0 RENT~
Syntax: (not applicable)

There is no RENT in this game.  Simply SAVE and QUIT when you want to
leave; your character and equipment are stored automatically.  You will
return to the same room next time you log in.

See also: QUIT, SAVE
~

0 RECALL /~
Syntax: RECALL

RECALL prays to the gods for miraculous transportation from where you are
back to the Temple of Devota in Dresden.  '/' is a synonym for RECALL.

If you RECALL during combat, you will lose experience (more than for fleeing),
and you will have a chance of failing (again, more than for fleeing).  This
chance is based on your recall skill, although a 100% recall does not
insure success.

RECALL costs half of your movement points.

RECALL doesn't work in certain god-forsaken rooms.  Characters afflicted by a
curse may not recall at all.

See also: WORD OF RECALL, PORTAL, MOVEMENT
~

0 REMORT~
Syntax: REMORT <password> <class> <guild> <race>

REMORT allows a character at their current maximum level to start over as a
reborn adventurer of the same name.  Each remort raises your permanent maximum
level by one, increases your starting and per-level stats, and grants extra
starting resources.  You may remort up to five times.

REQUIREMENT
  You must reach level 54 + (number of remorts already done) to remort.
  Example: first remort requires level 54; second requires level 55; etc.

WHAT YOU KEEP vs. WHAT YOU LOSE
  Keep: character name, gold (held and banked), quest points, remort bonuses,
        titles earned.  On your FIFTH (final) remort you also keep all gear.
  Lose: all levels (restart at level 3), all spells and skills, all group
        knowledge, current class/guild/race (replaced by your new choices).
        Stats are reset to 13 in all categories.

BENEFITS PER REMORT
  The table below shows cumulative values AT THE START of each new life.
  "Base HP/Mana/Move" is your starting maximum before any level gains.
  "Per Level Bonus" is added to every normal level-up gain.

  Remort   Req.Lvl   Max Lvl   Base HP/Mana/Move   Pracs   Trains   Per-Lvl
  ------   -------   -------   ----------------    -----   ------   -------
    1st       54        55           200 each          17      10       +1
    2nd       55        56           400 each          19      12       +2
    3rd       56        57           600 each          21      14       +3
    4th       57        58           800 each          23      16       +4
    5th       58        59          1000 each          25      18       +5

  Starting at remort 1 you are immune to hunger and thirst forever.
  Starting at remort 2 you gain psionic powers: you receive 4 randomly chosen
  skills from each of 4 psionic disciplines (16 total possible: ego whip,
  mindblast, nightmare, torment, clairvoyance, mindbar, psionic armor, psychic
  shield, telekinesis, astral walk, shift, confuse, project, transfusion, mind
  leech, enervate).  These are re-granted on each subsequent remort since
  skills are wiped.  At the final (5th) remort all 16 psionic skills are given.
  Starting at remort 4 your recall always succeeds (no skill check, no
  random-room mishap).  Recall from combat still costs experience.
  On the 5th (final) remort you carry unlimited items and weight and may
  choose any class and guild freely (the usual restriction of picking only
  classes you have never been does not apply).

HERO QUEST COST
  Buying level 51 (hero) via AQUEST BUY HERO costs:
    7000 quest points for a character who has never remorted.
    5000 quest points for any character who has remorted at least once.

QUEST SHOP ITEMS
  These items can be purchased from the quest master (AQUEST BUY <item>):

  POTION OF POWER (450 qp)  — buy: AQUI BUY EMPOWER  or  AQUI BUY POWER
    Bestows Sanctuary, Haste (+DEX), and Flight for the duration of the
    cast.  A single vial of extraordinary divine energy.

  POTION OF THE GIANT (450 qp)  — buy: AQUI BUY GIANT
    Grants Giant Strength, Stone Skin fortification, and the ability to
    Pass Door.  Brewed from ancient mountain earth and raw elemental force.

  SCROLL OF FARSLAY (600 qp)  — buy: AQUI BUY FARSLAY  or  AQUI BUY SCROLL
    A black scroll inscribed with the ancient rite of Farslay.  Calls
    divine vengeance down upon all enemies in the room.  One use only.

  POTION OF SANCTUARY (150 qp)  — buy: AQUI BUY SANCTUARY
    A more modest blessing that wraps the drinker in a protective aura.

  PRACTICES (500 qp)  — buy: AQUI BUY PRAC
    Awards 1-3 extra practice sessions for training skills.

CLASS, GUILD, AND RACE OPTIONS
  You must choose a class AND guild combination you have never used before
  (tracked across all remorts) -- EXCEPT on your final (5th) remort.
  Classes: mage, cleric, thief, warrior, monk, necro
  Guilds:  mage, cleric, thief, warrior, none
           (Monks and Necros must use 'none' as their guild.)
  Races:   human, elf, dwarf, hobbit, saurian
           Monks are restricted to: dwarf, human.
           Necromancers are restricted to: elf, human.

NOTES
  If you supply a class with no guild (monk or necro), type 'none' for guild.
  All affects, immunities, resistances, and flags are cleared on remort.
  Your pk_state is reset; the title is set to match your new class.
  Remort history is saved; HELP SCORE shows your remort count.

See also: HERO, HEROLEVELS, GAIN, GROUPS
~

0 AFFECT~
Syntax: affect

Shows all magical and psionic effects currently active on your character.
For each effect, the entry shows the spell or skill name, the modifier it
applies, the stat or attribute affected, and how many ticks remain.  A
duration of "permanent" means the effect does not expire.

See also: SCORE, IDENTIFY, ATTRIBUTE
~

0 ATTRIBUTE ATTRIBUTES~
Syntax: attribute

Displays a quick summary of your five core attributes: Strength,
Intelligence, Wisdom, Dexterity, and Constitution.  Values are shown as
base(current) to separate your trained score from bonuses applied by
equipment or active spells.

See also: SCORE, AFFECT, TRAIN
~

0 REPORT~
Syntax: report

Announces your current hit points, mana, and movement to yourself and to
every player in the same room.  Useful in group combat so healers and
allies can gauge how much support you need.  Does not reveal your
maximum values, only your current condition.

See also: SCORE, GROUP, HEAL
~

0 SCORE~
Syntax: score

Displays your full character sheet: name, level, class, race, all
attributes, hit points, mana, movement, armor class, hitroll, damroll,
experience, gold, remort count, and more.  Attribute scores are shown as
trained(current) so you can see exactly how much bonus gear and spells
are contributing.  Only you can see your own SCORE output.

See also: ATTRIBUTE, AFFECT, WORTH, REPORT
~

0 WORTH~
Syntax: worth

Shows a brief financial and experience summary: your current gold on hand,
bank balance, total experience earned, and how much experience remains
before your next level.  A quick alternative to the full SCORE display
when you only need to check progress or wealth.

See also: SCORE, BANK, BALANCE
~

0 SLEEP~
Syntax: sleep

SLEEP puts your character into a deep slumber.  While asleep you
regenerate hit points, mana, and movement at the fastest possible rate,
but you are more vulnerable to attack and will not hear most events in
the room.  Use WAKE or STAND to get up.

For information on the sleep spell, see SLEEPSPELL.

See also: REST, STAND, WAKE, WIMPY
~

0 REST STAND WAKE SIT~
Syntax: rest
Syntax: stand
Syntax: wake
Syntax: sit

These commands change your position.  When you REST or SIT, you regenerate
hit points, mana, and movement faster than while standing, while remaining
aware of your surroundings.

STAND or WAKE returns you to a standing position.  You can also use WAKE
to rouse a sleeping character.

See also: SLEEP, FLEE, WIMPY
~

0 RUN~
Syntax: run <direction> [distance]

RUN moves repeatedly in one direction.  Distance is optional; it defaults to
30 and is capped at 30.  Movement can stop early at a wall, closed exit, trap,
combat, or any other condition that prevents normal movement.  All ten normal
directions and their abbreviations are accepted.

See also: SPEEDWALK, RECALL, AUTOEXIT, EXITS
~

0 SPEEDWALK~
Syntax: speedwalk <route>

SPEEDWALK follows compact lowercase direction tokens.  Put an optional count
before each direction; each count defaults to 1 and is capped at 30.

Examples: speedwalk 3n2e1s
          speedwalk 2ne4w1d

Valid tokens are n, e, s, w, u, d, ne, nw, se, and sw.  Movement still uses
normal exits and costs, and stops if combat begins or you cannot continue.

See also: RUN, EXITS, AUTOEXIT
~

0 SACRIFICE~
Syntax: sacrifice <object>

SACRIFICE offers an object to the gods, who may reward you.
The nature of the reward depends upon the type of object.

See also: JUNK, DONATE, AUTOSAC
~

0 SCAN~
Syntax: scan

The SCAN command tells you what mobs are in surrounding rooms.  Only visible
mobs will be shown, unless you are affected by the appropriate detection
spells.  You cannot scan through a closed door.

See also: SEARCH, TRACK, EXITS
~

0 SCROLL~
Syntax: scroll
Syntax: scroll <number>

This command changes the number of lines the mud sends you in a page (the
default is 24 lines).  Change this to a higher number for larger screen
sizes, or to 0 to disable paging.

See also: BRIEF, COMPACT, PROMPT
~

0 SCROLL OF FARSLAY FARSLAY~
The Scroll of Farslay is purchasable from the quest master for 600 quest
points (AQUEST BUY FARSLAY).

When read, it calls down the ancient rite of divine vengeance upon all
enemies within range.  The scroll delivers three successive castings of the
Vengence spell for massive area damage.  One use only.

To use: hold the scroll in your inventory and type READ SCROLL.

See also: AQUEST, POTION OF POWER, POTION OF THE GIANT
~

0 POTION OF POWER~
The Potion of Power is purchasable from the quest master for 450 quest
points (AQUEST BUY EMPOWER or AQUEST BUY POWER).

Drinking it grants three powerful protective effects simultaneously:
  Sanctuary  — envelope the body in a divine shield (halves damage taken).
  Haste      — greatly increases attack speed and Dexterity.
  Fly        — lifts the drinker off the ground.

All effects last for the standard spell duration at level 60.

See also: AQUEST, POTION OF THE GIANT, SCROLL OF FARSLAY
~

0 POTION OF THE GIANT~
The Potion of the Giant is purchasable from the quest master for 450 quest
points (AQUEST BUY GIANT).

Drinking it applies three combat enhancement effects simultaneously:
  Giant Strength — dramatically boosts Strength and damage output.
  Stone Skin     — hardens the skin, significantly improving armor class.
  Pass Door      — allows movement through closed (non-locked) doors.

All effects last for the standard spell duration at level 60.

See also: AQUEST, POTION OF POWER, SCROLL OF FARSLAY
~

0 SKILLS SPELLS~
Syntax: skills
Syntax: spells

The skills and spells commands are used to display your character's list
of available skills (or spells, as the case may be).  They are listed in
order of level, with mana cost (for spells) or percentage (for skills)
listed where applicable.

See also: GAIN, GROUPS, PRACTICE
~

0 SPLIT~
Syntax: split <amount>

SPLIT splits some gold between you and all the members of your
group who are in the same room as you.  It's customary to SPLIT
the gold after a kill.  However, this is most conveniently done using
the AUTOSPLIT option.

See also: AUTOSPLIT, GROUP, GIVE
~

0 TIME~
Syntax: time

Displays the current in-game time of day, the date since the mud was last
started, and the real-world local time on the host server.  Game time
progresses faster than real time; day and night cycles affect some spells,
monster behavior, and room lighting.

See also: WEATHER, TICK
~

0 WEATHER~
Syntax: weather

Shows the current in-game weather conditions: sky state (clear, cloudy,
raining, storming, etc.), wind direction, and temperature.  Weather can
affect movement, some spells, and area events.  Certain seasons also
change which weather patterns are possible.

See also: TIME, TICK
~

0 TITLE~
Syntax: title <string>

Sets your title to a given string.

The game supplies a title when your character is created and when you
advance a level.  You can use TITLE to set your title to something else.

See also: DESCRIPTION, SCORE, WHO
~

0 TRAIN~
Syntax: train <str int wis dex con hp mana>

TRAIN increases one of your attributes.  When you start the game, your
character has standard attributes based on your class, and several
initial training sessions.  You can increase your attributes by
using these sessions at a trainer.  Trainers can be found in mud school,
wandering town or in guilds.

It takes one training session to improve an attribute, or to increase
mana or hp by 10.  You receive one training session per level.  Training
sessions are also used to buy new skills and practices may be converted to
trains to help you in your studies.  See help on GAIN for more information.

The best attributes to train first are WIS and CON.  WIS gives you more
practices when you gain a level and  CON gives you more hit points.
In the long run, your character will be most powerful if you train
both WIS and CON to their maximum (which depends on your race and class)
before training anything else.

See also: PRACTICE, GAIN, SCORE, ATTRIBUTE
~

0 VISIBLE~
Syntax: visible

VISIBLE cancels your hiding and sneaking, as well as any invisibility,
making you visible again.

See also: HIDE, SNEAK, STEALTH
~

0 WHERE~
Syntax: where
Syntax: where <character>

WHERE without an argument tells you the location of visible players in the same
area as you are.

WHERE with an argument tells you the location of one character with that name
within your area, including monsters.

See also: WHO, SCAN, AREAS
~

0 WHO WHOIS~
Syntax: who
Syntax: who <level-range>
Syntax: who <class or race>
Syntax: who <class or race> <level-range>
Syntax: whois <character>

WHO shows the people currently in the game.  Some people may choose to
remain unseen and won't appear when you use WHO.

WHOIS provides information about a specific player, including their class
and level.  That information may be useful in determining whether to respond
to a request to group or in ascertaining that the person speaking to you is
an immortal and should, therefore, really not be ignored.

WHO without any arguments shows all of the (visible) people currently playing.

With arguments, WHO can show players of certain classes or levels.
For example:

who 10          lists all players of level 10 or above
who 15 20       lists all players between level 15 and 20
who cleric      lists all clerics playing
who immortal    lists all immortals playing
who cleric 1 5  lists all clerics between levels 1 and 5
who elf cleric  lists all elven clerics
who <castle>    lists all members of a specified castle

Classes and races may be abbreviated.

See also: WHERE, FINGER, SCORE
~

0 WIMPY~
Syntax: wimpy
Syntax: wimpy <number>

WIMPY sets your wimpy value.  When your character takes damage that reduces
your hit points below your wimpy value, you will automatically attempt to flee.
You will only flee if your character is not in a wait state -- i.e. has not
been using combat commands like cast, trip and bash, and has not been
tripped or bash by an enemy.

WIMPY with no argument sets your wimpy value to 20% of your maximum hit points.

Some monsters are wimpy.

See also: FLEE, RECALL, SCORE
~

0 !~
Syntax: !

! repeats the last command you typed.

See also: COMMANDS
~

0 NORTH SOUTH EAST WEST UP DOWN NORTHEAST NORTHWEST SOUTHEAST SOUTHWEST~
Syntax: north
Syntax: south
Syntax: east
Syntax: west
Syntax: up
Syntax: down
Syntax: northeast
Syntax: ne
Syntax: northwest
Syntax: nw
Syntax: southeast
Syntax: se
Syntax: southwest
Syntax: sw

Use these commands to walk in a particular direction.  The ordinary directions
are usually abbreviated as N, S, E, W, U and D.   The diagonal directions
may be abbreviated as NE, NW, SE and SW.  If you are using a mud client to
access the game, you may need to alter some of the files to allow for the
abbreviated directions.

See also: EXITS, OPEN, MOVEMENT
~

0 BUG IDEA TYPO~
Syntax: bug <message>
Syntax: idea <message>
Syntax: typo <message>

These commands are essentially an artifact of mud history.  They still
exist and record a lot of bugs, ideas and typos to the implementors, but
it is preferable to use the bug and idea note forums.  See help NOTE for
more information.

See also: NOTE
~

0 GOSSIP .~
Syntax: gossip <message>
Syntax: gossip
Syntax: . <message>

GOSSIP broadcasts a message to all players in the world who have the channel
enabled.  The period (.) is a shortcut for GOSSIP.  This is the most
frequently used public channel on ToC.  Typing GOSSIP without a message
toggles the channel off; type it again to restore it.

You must be at least level 2 to use the gossip channel.

See also: SHOUT, YELL, CHANNELS, DEAF, QUIET, TELL
~

0 SHOUT~
Syntax: shout <message>

SHOUT sends a message to all awake players in the world.  To curb excessive
shouting, SHOUT imposes a three-second delay on the shouter.  Players must
be at least level 3 to shout.  Use DEAF to block incoming shouts, or QUIET
to silence all public channels at once.

See also: GOSSIP, YELL, DEAF, CHANNELS
~

0 YELL~
Syntax: yell <message>

YELL broadcasts a message to all awake players within your current area
only.  It is narrower than SHOUT (world-wide) and more broadly heard
than SAY (room only).

See also: SHOUT, GOSSIP, SAY, CHANNELS
~

0 INFO~
Syntax: info

INFO is a broadcast channel that displays automatic notifications when a
player attains a new level.  Typing INFO alone toggles the channel off or
on; you cannot send messages on the info channel directly.

See also: LEVELING, CHANNELS
~

0 LEVELING~
Syntax: leveling <message>
Syntax: leveling

The LEVELING channel is for congratulatory messages to players who just
leveled up.  INFO displays the automatic level notification; LEVELING is
where other players respond with congratulations.  Typing LEVELING alone
toggles the channel.

See also: INFO, GOSSIP, CHANNELS
~

0 MUSIC~
Syntax: music <message>
Syntax: music

The MUSIC channel is used for singing and sharing music-related content
with all players.  Send a message or type MUSIC alone to toggle it off or
on.

See also: GOSSIP, CHANNELS
~

0 DEAF~
Syntax: deaf

Toggles the shout channel on or off.  When deaf is active you will not
receive SHOUT messages from other players.  Type DEAF again to restore
your ability to hear shouts.  The gossip and other channels are unaffected;
use QUIET to silence everything at once.

See also: QUIET, CHANNELS, SHOUT
~

0 QUIET~
Syntax: quiet

Toggles all public channels off simultaneously, so you hear only EMOTES
and SAY in your current room.  Useful for concentrating during difficult
combat or roleplay scenes.  Type QUIET again to restore all channels.
Individual channel states are preserved; they return to how they were.

See also: DEAF, CHANNELS, NOISY
~

0 TELL~
Syntax: tell <character> <message>

TELL sends a private message to one awake player anywhere in the world.
Only the target sees your message.  Use REPLY to respond to the last tell
you received.

See also: REPLY, SAY, GTELL, CHANNELS
~

0 REPLY~
Syntax: reply <message>

REPLY sends a message to the last player who sent you a TELL.  REPLY
works even if you cannot see the player and without revealing their
identity.  This is handy for talking to invisible or switched immortal
players.

See also: TELL, SAY, GTELL
~

10 CGOS CASTLECHAT CC -~
Syntax: cgos <message>
Syntax: cc <message>

CGOS allows you to send a message to members of all castles.

CC is used to send  message only to members of your castle.  The dash, '-'
is a synonym for CC.

Type a channel name without a message to turn that channel off.

See also: CHANNELS, GOSSIP, TELL
~

51 HERO =~
Syntax: hero <message>

A channel is provided for heroes to communicate with one another.  This is
similar to the other public channels, like gossip, etc. The equals sign,
'=' is a synonym for hero.

Immortals may also use the hero channel.

See also: GOSSIP, CHANNELS, HEROQUEST
~

0 CHANNELS~
Syntax: channels

The CHANNELS command is used to tell you the status (on or off) of all of
your communications channels, e.g. gossip, shouts, info, etc..

To toggle the on/off status of a channel, type the name of that channel.

See also: GOSSIP, SHOUT, TELL, SAY, DEAF, QUIET
~

63 AREAVNUMS~
